#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078187 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078468 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d905f41-5940-547e-b3d2-558c6b881b68" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term in an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "eec455b0-98ff-59a5-b552-3381fb893fd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14392" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $42$ in the following arithmetic sequence?\n$-10, \\: -6, \\: -2, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a_1=-10$ and\n\n$$\nd=-6-(-10)=4\n$$\n\nwe can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-10 + (n - 1)(4) \\\\\n&=-10 + 4n - 4 \\\\\n&= 4n - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=42$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 4n - 14 \\\\\n42 &= 4n - 14 \\\\\n56 &= 4n \\\\\n14 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42$ is the $14$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078188 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c61d587-2441-5e8c-8da1-5bd62ff98e1e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "ff198a0e-d34e-5706-ad74-dbfa20fc3a94" :question/math-academy-id "e-14395" :question/problem "Given the arithmetic sequence\n\n$a_{n+1} = a_n + 12, \\quad a_1 = 3,\\quad n\\geq 1$,\nwhat is the index of the term $255$?" :question/worked-solution "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255$.\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n +{\\color{black}d}\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have\n\n$$\nd={\\color{black}12}\n$$\n\nWe are also told that\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{black}3}\n$$\n\nso we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 3 + (n - 1)(12) \\\\\n&= 3 + 12n - 12 \\\\\n&= 12n - 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 12n - 9 \\\\\n255 &= 12n - 9 \\\\\n264 &= 12n \\\\\n\\frac{264}{12} &= n \\\\\n22 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $255$ is the $22$nd term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078189 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bbbc551-e3ff-5cce-a572-e5b89067005a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given Another Term and the Common Difference"}] :question/id #uuid "84266116-5a1b-528a-929a-40b5d863e5a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14397" :question/problem "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_4=5$ and common difference $d=3$, what is the index of the term $95$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $a_4=5$ is $3$ jumps after $a_1$, we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1} + 3d \\\\\n5 &= a_{1} + 3(3) \\\\\n5 &= a_{1} + 9 \\\\\n- 4 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-4 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&=-4 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=95$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 7 \\\\\n95 &= 3n - 7 \\\\\n102 &= 3n \\\\\n\\frac{102}{3} &= n \\\\\n34 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $95$ is the $34$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078191 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d979a7bd-42a1-5d4f-8717-1caa06ce623c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given Two Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "5b905b18-5ac3-5a0f-a42f-1d9e53e792cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14399" :question/problem "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_2=12$ and $a_{4} = 10$, what is the index of the term $-12$?" :question/worked-solution "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term $a_1$.\n\nSince we are given $a_2=12$ and $a_4=10$, we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{4} - a_{2}}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 - 12}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $a_2=12$ is $1$ jump after $a_1$, we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1} + d \\\\\n12 &= a_{1} - 1 \\\\\n13 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 13 + (n - 1)(-1) \\\\\n&= 14 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=-12$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 14 - n \\\\\n- 12 &= 14 - n \\\\\n- 26 &= -n \\\\\n26 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-12$ is the $26$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c61d587-2441-5e8c-8da1-5bd62ff98e1e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "31aeabda-61fa-58f0-a30e-ee7377fc5759" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075711}] :question/id #uuid "cd8712dc-cbd7-5694-8112-15a3c2418d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-214198" :question/problem "Given the arithmetic sequence\n$a_{n + 1} = a_{n} - 6, a_{1} = 2, n \\ge 1$,\nthe index of the term $-124$ is $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d979a7bd-42a1-5d4f-8717-1caa06ce623c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given Two Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "60026508-8aeb-53d1-b9a9-b02a4098935f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075712}] :question/id #uuid "34bf3da3-eeb6-5d73-91c4-1ece08cbe280" :question/math-academy-id "q-214239" :question/problem "If an arithmetic sequence has $a_{6} = 12$ and $a_{9} = 24$, find the value of $n$ such that $a_{n} = 132$.$n =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
